/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - The quality factor of a reactive part is Q = 2π × (peak energy stored) ÷ (energy lost per cycle).
   - An on-chip inductor is a flat spiral of metal.
   - ω = 2πf is the angular frequency in rad/s; L is in H; Rs is in Ω.
   - As frequency rises, Cp carries more current.
   - fSR is in Hz.
   - Including Cp, the inductor's useful Q is Qeff ≈ (ωL/Rs)[1 − Rs²Cp/L − (f/fSR)²].
   - A metal-insulator-metal (MIM) capacitor uses two metal layers with a thin insulator between them.
   - C is the capacitance in F; Rs is the series resistance of the plates, vias and (for a MOS part) the channel, in Ω; ω = 2πf in rad/s.
   - Capacitor Q falls as frequency rises, the opposite of the inductor.
   - A varactor is a capacitor whose value is set by a DC control voltage.
*/
