/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - A resistor at temperature T delivers available noise power kTB into a matched load over a bandwidth B.
   - A two-port block (amplifier, mixer or filter) adds noise of its own, so its output signal-to-noise ratio is lower than its input ratio.
   - both ratios are power ratios in the same bandwidth, with the source at T₀ = 290 K.
   - Noise figure is the same quantity in decibels, NF = 10 log₁₀ F, and back again F = 10NF/10.
   - A passive lossy part at T₀ (a cable, filter or switch) with loss L as a power ratio has G = 1/L and F = L.
   - F is the noise factor of the whole chain; F₁, F₂, F₃ are the stage noise factors and G₁, G₂ the available power gains, all linear ratios, never dB.
   - Read it as noise referred to the input.
   - The design rule follows at once: put a low-noise amplifier with real gain first.
   - Sensitivity is the weakest input that still gives the demodulator the signal-to-noise ratio it needs.
   - Pmin is in dBm; −174 dBm/Hz is kT₀; B is the noise bandwidth of the channel in Hz; NF is the receiver noise figure in dB; SNRmin is the demodulator's requirement in dB.
*/
