/* book Worked example 2: Q: A satellite receiver's antenna has TA = 100 K. A cable with 2 dB loss at 290 K connects it to an LNA (NF = 1 dB, G = 20 dB), followed by the rest of the receiver with NF = 10 dB. Find the receiver noise temperature, the system noise temperature and the sensitivity in 1 MHz for SNRmin = 8 dB. How much does moving the LNA to the antenna, ahead of the cable, gain?
   1. Cable: L = 100.2 = 1.585, Tc = (1.585 − 1) × 290 = 170 K.
   2. LNA: TLNA = (100.1 − 1) × 290 = 75.1 K; G = 100. Rest: Tr = (10 − 1) × 290 = 2610 K.
   3. Cable first: Te = Tc + L·TLNA + L·Tr/G = 170 + 1.585 × 75.1 + 1.585 × 2610/100 = 170 + 119 + 41.4 = 330 K (NF = 3.30 dB).
   4. Tsys = TA + Te = 100 + 330 = 430 K.
   5. Noise floor: kTsysB = 1.38 × 10⁻²³ × 430 × 10⁶ = 5.94 × 10⁻¹⁵ W = −112.3 dBm; Pmin = −112.3 + 8 = −104.3 dBm.
   6. LNA first: Te = 75.1 + 170/100 + 1.585 × 2610/100 = 75.1 + 1.7 + 41.4 = 118 K (NF = 1.48 dB); Tsys = 218 K; Pmin = −107.2 dBm.
   Answer: Cable first: Te = 330 K, Tsys = 430 K, Pmin = −104.3 dBm. LNA at the antenna: Te = 118 K, Pmin = −107.2 dBm, 2.9 dB better, which is why masthead LNAs exist. */
