/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Model a memoryless amplifier as y = a₁x + a₂x² + a₃x³, where x is the input voltage, y the output voltage, a₁ the small-signal gain and a₂, a₃ constants.
   - On a dB plot of output power against input power, the small-signal line has slope 1: 1 dB more input gives 1 dB more output.
   - all powers in dBm; G is the small-signal gain in dB.
   - Setting the gain drop to 1 dB in the cubic model gives A²1dB = 0.145|a₁/a₃|.
   - In a two-tone test two equal tones at f₁ and f₂, each of power Pin, enter the amplifier.
   - Extend both straight lines: they cross at the third-order intercept point.
   - OIM3 is the output power of each IM3 product in dBm; Pout = Pin + G is the output per tone in dBm.
   - A measurement gives IIP3 directly.
   - In a chain, input intercepts combine as powers in milliwatts: 1/IIP3 = 1/IIP3₁ + G₁/IIP3₂ + G₁G₂/IIP3₃ + …, with gains as linear ratios.
   - The spurious-free dynamic range (SFDR) runs from the input-referred noise floor N up to the per-tone input at which the input-referred IM3 just equals N.
*/
