/* book exam traps: Treating IIP3 as a safe input. It is an extrapolated point about 10 dB above P1dB,in; an amplifier driven there is deep in compression. | Mixing input and output intercepts: OIP3 = IIP3 + G. Using OIP3 in the input form 3Pin − 2·IIP3 shifts the IM3 answer by 2G. | Writing P1dB,out = P1dB,in + G. The measured output at compression is 1 dB below that: P1dB,out = P1dB,in + G − 1. | Combining intercepts like Friis with the first stage dominant. For IIP3 the later stages are multiplied by the gain ahead of them, so they dominate. | Using an output noise floor with IIP3 in the SFDR formula. Both must be referred to the same point. */
